Baseline PBR shading, atmosphere and surface detail
==================================================

Scope and shared contracts
  Improve baseline physically based rendering with and without LumOn. Provide a physically based
  sky, optional height/depth-map tessellation and relief mapping, PBR liquids, an investigation of
  approximate underwater caustics, and volumetric clouds.
  Keep standalone PBR usable without LumOn resources or indirect-lighting production. Standalone
  environmental lighting should keep outdoor shadows readable without injecting sunlight through
  occluders or applying an indiscriminate brightness floor to enclosed spaces.
  These are implementation tasks; algorithm choices and performance targets require investigation.
  Reuse existing shader/resource owners, compiled SPIR-V contracts, demand loading and settings.
  Keep optional features bounded, configurable and independently disableable.
  This list owns baseline PBR material improvements. Surface Cache performance
  and geometry work remain in docs/LumOn.SurfaceCache.PerformanceAndMaterials.todo.

Tasks

  ☐ Profile and optimize ambient-occlusion GPU cost while preserving visibility quality.
    - Bounds-arithmetic candidates were rejected: uncontended captured-scene horizon cost improved
      about 13-15%, but full-resolution synthetic cases consistently regressed about 10-50%.
      Original production bounds are restored; captured-input and synthetic comparison fixtures remain.
      The reference reproduces event 7091's original output bit-for-bit. Two full bounds replacements
      passed exact-image checks but failed performance qualification; the ceiling-only variant passed
      22 image cases and was not timed before changing investigation direction.
      Evaluate reducing hierarchy source-recovery fetches, including per-parent minimum-child masks
      that preserve query-dependent ties. Measure added build/storage cost and the full AO path before
      selecting an implementation. Matched live scenes, both lighting modes and user acceptance remain open.
      Implementation/evidence: docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md / Horizon-integrated ambient visibility.
    - Starting evidence: the 2026-10-11 performance-issue.rdc capture at 3440x1377 has repeatable
      AO replay timings of 2.71-2.72 ms, dominated by the half-resolution horizon pass at event 7091
      (~2.61 ms). These are optimization leads, not proof of the reported 120+ to 90 FPS regression;
      inconsistent engine-draw replay spikes invalidate total replay frame-time comparisons.
      Extracted evidence: artifacts/RenderDocPerformance/timings_repeat_*.json and pipelines.json.
    - Establish matched steady-state CPU/GPU and live frame-time baselines across native AO quality,
      resolution, representative outdoor/interior/foliage scenes and LumOn enabled/disabled. Record
      settings, hardware and build configuration; measure shared DepthHierarchy once, then horizon,
      filtering and reconstruction separately. Compare AO enabled/disabled to isolate its contribution.
    - Profile horizon candidate sampling, hierarchy descent, texture bandwidth and integration work;
      evaluate reuse, locality and redundant-work removal before selecting an optimization. Reuse
      existing AO, depth-hierarchy, shader and resource owners; retain native quality/enable authority.
    - Preserve running-horizon relaxation, projection and receiver-depth agreement, transmission,
      background rejection, edge-aware reconstruction and indirect-only lighting application.
      Any sampling/resolution tradeoff must be explicit, quality-bounded and visually accepted;
      do not count a silent reduction in quality as an equivalent-work speedup.
    - Validate changed behavior through focused subagent numerical/GPU tests and independent review;
      obtain user-run visual acceptance for contact shadows, thin geometry, foliage, screen edges,
      movement and disocclusion. Check resize, reload, runtime settings and both lighting modes.
    - Record matched before/after pass and live frame costs, bandwidth/storage changes and remaining
      limits. Remove temporary profiling instrumentation; mark complete only with measured improvement
      and preserved output contracts. Contract: docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md / Horizon-integrated ambient visibility.

    Optimization evaluations
      Each evaluation inherits the parent task's settings, lifecycle, lighting and acceptance contracts.
      Evaluate alternatives independently against the frozen reference before combining successful changes.
      Record measured costs, output differences and a retain/reject decision; an evaluation may finish
      with rejection, but the parent optimization task still requires a measured, accepted improvement.

      ☑ Attribute source-recovery, integration and repeated-lookup costs before selecting candidates.
        - Evidence: two Release diagnostic batches passed all ten captured/synthetic workloads, with
          identical request histograms and image checksums. At the user's request the game stayed open;
          timings are contended attribution leads, not additive costs or performance qualification.
          Source-descent removal consistently reduced cost; integration removal had mixed effects.
          Exact repeated requests were only 0-0.2023% of calls. Prioritize minimum-child masks,
          deprioritize per-receiver whole-lookup caching, and retain integration math as secondary.
          Tiling needs further locality/bandwidth evidence. Results: docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md;
          reproducible sources, raw samples and metadata: artifacts/AoAttribution.
          Temporary diagnostics removed; restored Release build passed and default AO comparisons
          passed 15 cases with 19 optional skips. Parent optimization acceptance remains open.
        - Use temporary diagnostic variants to estimate source-recovery versus integration cost and
          count repeated quantized sample coordinates per receiver across representative workloads.
        - Treat omitted-work timings as diagnostic leads, not additive cost decomposition or proof of
          equivalent quality; instrumentation can change shader scheduling, register use and caching.
        - Prioritize masks, repeated-lookup reuse and integration math from the evidence; evaluate
          tiling if memory access warrants it. Remove temporary instrumentation after recording results.

      ☑ Evaluate minimum-child masks for hierarchy source recovery.
        - Candidate evaluation: a nine-slot mask scan passed image checks but regressed flat and thin
          workloads. Ascending set-bit visitation passed the same checks and improved complete-AO
          paired timings across all ten workloads in two game-open batches. Captured paired median
          savings were 1.3373/1.3368 ms; these are contended prototype leads, not live FPS proof.
          Retain the accepted production implementation: the AO owner owns R16UI masks, leaving the
          shared LumOn R32F hierarchy unchanged. Actual-owner comparisons passed all ten workloads
          in both batches, with bit-identical raw/filtered/reconstructed output and captured paired
          savings of 1.3322/1.3327 ms. Small image/lifecycle/runtime checks passed 23 cases, including
          native disable and both LumOn modes. Cleaned Release build and independent source review
          passed; final focused suite passed 76 tests with 29 optional skips. Temporary diagnostics
          removed. On 2026-10-11 the user reported an improvement to around 90-110 FPS, versus
          the earlier approximately 90 FPS report, and no visible difference in the areas checked.
          The user explicitly accepted the candidate, supported by exact-image and automated lifecycle
          coverage, without an exhaustive manual matrix. This completes the mask evaluation; controlled
          live frame-time distributions and broader scene coverage remain with the parent task.
          Evidence: artifacts/AoMinimumChildMasks /
          production-summary.json and final-focused; user report in the task conversation.
        - Build masks of all minimum-depth children and preserve query-dependent nearest-child ties,
          visitation order and conservative odd/collapsed-axis footprints. Keep the shared R32F
          hierarchy contract used by LumOn; do not substitute one preselected source coordinate.
        - Measure mask generation, physical storage, bindings and bandwidth alongside saved lookup
          cost. Respect existing hardware limits and retire optional resources when no longer needed.
        - Require bit-identical horizon images against the reference and measure hierarchy-plus-AO
          cost, including the captured scene and synthetic cases that rejected the bounds rewrites.

      ☐ Evaluate reuse of repeated sample lookups within each AO receiver.
        - Measure duplicate rates first. Reuse source recovery only for equivalent quantized sample
          coordinates, footprint levels and inputs; preserve ordered horizon updates for every sample.
        - Compare saved reads against lookup overhead, added registers and spills. Keep reuse bounded
          and local to the current receiver; validate bit-identical output and total pass cost.

      ☐ Evaluate horizon integration math using vector projections.
        - Investigate eliminating redundant angle/trigonometric conversions while preserving signed
          horizon selection, limits, relaxation, transmission weighting and integration behavior.
        - Cover grazing angles, zero/near-zero vectors, horizon boundaries and both quality settings.
          Quantify numerical and complete-image differences against the reference; define and justify
          any nonzero tolerance explicitly and obtain visual acceptance before retaining changed output.

      ☐ Evaluate spatially tiled AO processing for data reuse and locality.
        - Measure depth/material locality and footprint reach before selecting tile size or execution
          layout. Account for wide footprints, tile halos, odd extents, boundaries and background work.
        - Compare shared-memory/register pressure, occupancy, synchronization and extra preparation
          against saved reads. Preserve sampling and output contracts; include every added pass in
          timing and test hardware limits and resource lifecycle through the existing owners.

      ☐ Evaluate adaptive sampling with explicit visibility-quality bounds.
        - Investigate reducing samples in smooth regions while retaining evidence near discontinuities;
          validate the classifier itself against thin geometry, foliage, corners and distant receivers.
        - Measure classification overhead and complete AO cost against the fixed-sample reference.
          Record quality differences, spatial instability and missed occluders; require user acceptance
          and retain native quality authority. Do not report reduced sampling as equivalent-work savings.

      ☐ Evaluate temporal accumulation with reduced per-frame sampling.
        - Assess motion/reprojection inputs and history ownership before selecting a design. Include
          depth/normal rejection, disocclusion, camera cuts, moving geometry and confidence handling.
        - Measure history storage, reprojection/filter cost and total AO savings; check ghosting,
          flicker, convergence and responsiveness against the current-frame reference in motion.
        - Specify invalidation for resize, reload, world changes and runtime settings, plus behavior
          when valid history is unavailable. Require explicit visual acceptance of the temporal tradeoff
          and reconcile the current no-history contract in docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md before adoption.

  ☐ Optimize owned postprocessing submission and pass scheduling.
    - Starting evidence: the same capture's postprocessing interval contains 25 fullscreen draws,
      54 program switches, 108 sampler binds and 75 framebuffer binds, with ~0.28 ms repeatable GPU
      replay cost. Binding churn predates the shader lifecycle ownership refactor; its managed CPU
      cost and contribution to the FPS regression remain unmeasured. Evidence:
      artifacts/RenderDocPerformance/frame_events.json and timings_repeat_*.json.
    - Establish matched CPU submission/allocation, GPU and live frame-time baselines. Attribute costs
      to PostprocessDraw preparation/submission, shader activation, input publication, context checks,
      pass teardown and boundary restoration. Compare equivalent builds before/after the lifecycle
      refactor where reproducible; separate first-use preparation and diagnostics from steady state.
    - Reduce measured redundant program/sampler/framebuffer transitions through existing command,
      pipeline and state-cache owners. Evaluate retaining compatible state across owned passes and
      restoring engine state at the enclosing boundary; preserve complete restoration on normal,
      exception, nested and unavailable-resource paths and safe executable retirement.
    - Evaluate bloom reduction/filter/reconstruction scheduling and intermediate bandwidth for
      measured opportunities to reuse work or combine compatible passes. Preserve the selected kernel,
      scale weights and output quality; skip disabled-effect work through existing settings authority.
    - Preserve scene-linear HDR, exposure metering/adaptation order, bloom and shaft behavior, grading,
      exactly-once display conversion/dithering and UI/offscreen separation. Keep attachment ownership,
      feedback avoidance, resize/reload and both PBR lighting modes intact; coordinate shared changes
      with the AO task above and the water performance task below without duplicating their work.
    - Use focused subagent state-restoration, image/numerical and lifecycle tests plus independent
      review. Obtain user-run visual acceptance; record matched call counts, CPU/GPU/frame costs and
      allocation/storage changes. Do not use reduced call counts alone as completion evidence.
      Contract: docs/PBR.Postprocessing.md; retain results there and remove temporary diagnostics.

  ☑ Implement microfacet multiple-scattering energy compensation consistently across the PBR pipeline.
    User accepted in-game appearance on 2026-10-15. Before completion, the user requested
    an RG16F texture lookup and a shared Shading quality option. The refactor is complete:
    quality 0/1/2/3 selects 16/32/64/128 square texels, default 3, hardware
    bilinear clamp filtering and no mips. Existing full-precision CPU material calibration
    remains stable across quality changes. Earlier buffer and package receipts below are
    historical; testing uses the normal Debug launcher without a separate package.
    Final validation passes 448 distinct focused cases with no unresolved selected failures,
    including native sampler rebinding and live quality replacement. Both shader catalogs
    pass (450 production / 528 fixture variants); the normal Debug build and copied shader
    inputs match the tested assembly. Receipts: artifacts/MicrofacetEnergy/TextureLookupValidation.md
    and texture-default-debug-receipt.json. Default actual GPU errors against 360 independent
    references are 0.07937% relative E / 0.0005511 absolute Ef; the latter includes the
    separately qualified half-output filter precision allowance. Lower-tier tradeoffs and
    warm synthetic cost are documented in docs/PBR.MaterialColorAndDisplay.md.
    Initial investigation (2026-10-15): independent double-precision visible-normal and
    NDF integration matched the previous GGX/Schlick masking response.
    At roughness 0.04 and NdotV 0.001, previous E=0.0006296484 requires white-reflector
    W=1588.19. At NdotV 0.01 it still requires W=181.42. These are intrinsic losses of
    the previous masking model, not lookup noise. A 128x128 lookup with a warped view axis
    reached 0.474% maximum relative E error across 256 seeded off-grid checks, but interpolation
    accuracy does not remove those large gains or qualify storage bounds.
    User-approved height-correlated Smith visibility, using the same GGX distribution and
    Fresnel with algebraic cancellation instead of the additive Cook denominator epsilon,
    gives E=0.907625/W=1.10178 at the first case. Its sampled maximum W is 3.258891 across
    45 roughness/view cases. This is an approved model change, not equivalent arithmetic or
    a qualified global bound. Numerical sources/receipts: artifacts/MicrofacetEnergy.
    The default implementation now shares compensation across direct, environment, forward,
    liquid and diffuse-cache material routes. The retained 128x128 Float32 source table uses quadratic
    roughness and quartic view coordinates; independent corner/off-grid checks found maximum
    relative E error 0.03246% and absolute Ef error 0.000281. All 450 production shader variants
    compile; 260 distinct checks pass across overlapping runs, including GPU/table and white-furnace checks.
    Receipts: artifacts/MicrofacetEnergy/GpuValidation.md and StoragePrecision.md (104 CPU precision checks).
    The nine registered-consumer fixture gaps are now resolved using the real camera-owned
    frame freeze. Updated physical references include source calibration, cache material-byte
    encoding, radius attenuation and HDR emission units, retaining numerical tolerances.
    Runtime handoff correction passes 52/52 focused checks, including those nine cases and
    independent world/screen directional inputs plus full-image material assertions in both
    gather modes. The local-cache handoff previously encoded positive-alpha world radiance
    twice; it now decodes C=1/16 before interpolation and the caller's single encode.
    Receipt: artifacts/MicrofacetEnergy/runtime-handoff-corrected.trx. Six retained-lighting,
    reload/history and movement cases also pass (runtime-lifetime.trx). Final Release focused
    qualification passes 390/390 with zero skipped (release-focused.trx), including the 450
    production and 528 fixture shader variants. The user subsequently accepted in-game
    appearance; the subsequent texture/quality refactor is validated as recorded above.
    Five additional material-readiness cases now pass in Release after supplying their
    fixture-owned camera through the existing producer adapter, with original assertions
    unchanged. See artifacts/MicrofacetEnergy/nearfield-readiness-owned-frame.trx and
    RuntimeValidation.md. This fixture-only follow-up leaves production behavior unchanged.
    User-run acceptance uses the normal debug launcher and its default Debug build. The
    implementation and embedded lookup are unconditional; no separate package or feature flag
    is required. The archived Release ZIP and Candidate/receipt.json are build evidence only.
    The earlier buffer implementation's Debug receipt is default-debug-receipt.json;
    the current RG16F implementation uses texture-default-debug-receipt.json. Both are
    under artifacts/MicrofacetEnergy. No game was launched for headless checks.
    Practical highlight containment is now user-approved instead of widening transient formats:
    freeze camera-driven P with the retained faint-light floor, contain exceptional scene RGB at
    each destination range, and keep invalid persistent/cache/history rejection strict. Source
    estimates enforce coherence, not an unclipped-image certificate or scene-wide scale reduction.
    Runtime selection is implemented; 146 distinct containment/storage/EV100/history checks pass,
    with both 450/528 shader catalogs passing. Receipt:
    artifacts/MicrofacetEnergy/CameraContainmentValidation.md. No scene-lighting attachment formats, source powers,
    minimum roughness or emitter sizes change. Appearance was subsequently accepted. Numerical
    source estimates use 40M punctual for opaque, 42M for normalized liquid surfaces and 3
    times disk radiance. They are not a certified universal Float32 envelope or a requirement
    to preserve every extreme highlight under the approved containment policy.
    The earlier wider transient HDR evaluation remains historical evidence, not the selected
    implementation. Source inventory, selected highlight policy and logical memory costs are in
    docs/PBR.MicrofacetStorage.Evaluation.md. Persistent incident/diffuse targets remain separate.
    The completed photometric tasklist was removed
    in commit 863b6c7d; consult its preceding revision for historical qualification and the
    retained FrameLightingBounds/LightingStorage and scene/atmosphere/water contracts for
    current behavior. Do not recreate the retired plan or assume its bounds survive this change.
    Storage follow-up: joint numerical scans found compensated punctual response witnesses
    of 4.74 million (retained masking) and 39.26 million (stable correlated masking) per unit
    incident light, exceeding the old 66,560 certificate. Finite-disk mirror-cone witnesses
    also exceed the old 0.9-times-disk bound: 5.73 and 1.37 respectively. These sampled
    counterexamples disprove reuse of the old bounds; they are not replacement certificates.
    Camera-driven scale and separate faint-pixel retention are now checked together with
    actual exceptional-highlight containment. The user approved height-correlated Smith GGX explicitly.
    Consumer inventory: deferred direct and native forward/late/OIT routes share
    pbr_direct_brdf; liquids separately call the punctual and finite-disk helpers.
    Standalone composition and LumOn's receiver response share VgeEnvironmentSplit;
    forward LumOn's block-light fallback now uses that same split. Liquid environment
    reflection and interface transmission now share the compensated surface-energy budget.
    The current Surface Cache stores Lambertian diffuse outgoing radiance plus emission;
    it does not bake view-dependent microfacet specular. Therefore the extreme specular
    witnesses do not establish an existing persistent-cache overflow. Requalify actual
    cache/probe inputs and material weighting, keeping compensation at the receiver once,
    rather than multiplying already reflected cached outgoing lighting by W.
    - Use a directional-albedo lookup indexed by material roughness and NdotV, with reflectance E
      and its Fresnel-weighted term Ef. Apply the compensation W = 1 + F0 * (1 - E) / E and
      estimate compensated lobe reflectance as W * (E * F0 + Ef * (F90 - F0)). Retain explicit
      dielectric/metallic and grazing-reflectance contracts; bound low-E divisions and invalid inputs.
    - Generate and validate the lookup independently against the selected owned GGX distribution
      and masking model. Resolve any masking-model change explicitly; a lookup for a different
      BRDF must not silently compensate the existing one. Define resolution, precision, filtering,
      domain limits and integration error before choosing storage or an analytical replacement.
    - Share the compensation and lobe-energy partition across deferred, forward/late/OIT and
      liquid surface specular shading, including punctual and finite-disk emitters. Keep emitter-size
      compensation distinct from microfacet multiple scattering; neither replaces the other.
    - Reconcile environment/reflection shading and both LumOn modes with the same material energy
      model. Inventory cached versus receiver-evaluated responses so compensation is applied once.
      Replace or explicitly qualify the current approximate environment response where needed;
      do not blindly multiply already-integrated or compensated lighting by the direct-light factor.
    - Account for compensated specular reflectance when weighting diffuse, layered response and
      surface transmission, avoiding duplicated energy or inconsistent dielectric/metal behavior.
      Preserve separate water-volume scattering, atmosphere transport and source calibration.
    - Own the lookup through existing shader/binding/resource infrastructure, preserving GLSL 330,
      coherent publication, reload and retirement. Avoid new fullscreen passes or per-frame CPU
      integration; measure lookup bandwidth, shader cost and any persistent resource overhead.
    - Requalify photometric HDR/pre-exposure and persistent-lighting storage bounds and precision
      after compensation increases reflected radiance. For transient scenes, use the approved
      practical containment policy rather than requiring preservation of every extreme peak;
      validate camera-driven scale selection, destination containment and separate faint-pixel
      retention. Keep persistent/history validity strict. Coordinate with the retired photometric
      tasklist at 863b6c7d^ and the retained storage contracts identified above;
      do not claim this corrects the centered finite-disk integration discrepancy without evidence.
    - Run focused subagent numerical/GPU and lifecycle checks for white-furnace energy, reflectance
      extremes, roughness/grazing limits, lookup convergence and matching direct/environment response.
      Validate supported liquid/transmission and both lighting modes, then obtain user-run appearance
      acceptance. Document approximation and reciprocity limits, measured costs and retained behavior
      in docs/PBR.MaterialColorAndDisplay.md and the affected lighting/water ownership documents.

